    "https://apply.interfolio.com/187820": {
      "title": "Department of African Diaspora Studies (Tenure Track, Start Fall 2027)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-21 Location: Grinnell, IA The Department of African Diaspora Studies (ADS) invites applications for a tenure-track appointment beginning Fall 2027. Rank open, but Assistant Professor/Associate Professor preferred. Research and teaching interests might include, but are not limited to: environmental studies and Black ecologies; animal studies; film, media, and visual cultures; racial capitalism; Black political thought and social movements; and race, technology, and the digital. The teaching load is five courses over two semesters; periodically one course will be Tutorial (a topical writing/critical thinking course for first-year students, which is the only College-wide requirement beyond the completion of a major in an individually advised curriculum). This course load includes a 100-level introduction to African Diaspora Studies, a 200-level theory and methodologies course, other 200-level courses in the candidate’s area of specialization, and contributions to upper-level seminars and independent work . Assistant Professor/Associate Professor preferred. A letter of application, in which candidates should discuss their interest in developing as a teacher and scholar in a highly selective undergraduate liberal arts college that emphasizes close student-faculty interaction. They should also discuss their potential to contribute to a college community that maintains a diversity of people and perspectives as one of its core values. To be assured of full consideration, all application materials should be received by September 15, 2026. Candidates will need to upload a letter of application, curriculum vitae, a 2-page teaching statement, transcripts (copies are acceptable), and provide email addresses for three references. The candidate letter should help the search committee understand how their teaching and research engage core programmatic questions: What are the realities and possibilities of Black life in the African diaspora? What theories and techniques are most essential to engaging the realities and possibilities of Black life in the African diaspora? Questions about this search should be directed to the search chair, Professor John Thabiti Willis, at adssearches@grinnell.edu or 641-269-4803.",
      "department": "Grinnell College"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188085": {
      "title": "Department of Biology (Tenure Track, Start Fall 2027)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-23 Location: Grinnell, IA The Department of Biology invites applications for a tenure-track appointment in biology beginning Fall 2027. Assistant Professor (Ph.D.) preferred; Associate Professor possible. Research and teaching interests might include but are not limited to organismal biology and ecology, with the potential to engage with the college’s field station (the Conard Environmental Research Area ) and the Environmental Studies program, which will introduce a new major in 2027. The teaching load is five courses (with each lab section counting as ½ course) over two semesters; periodically one course will be Tutorial (a topical writing/critical thinking course for first-year students, the only College-wide requirement beyond the completion of a major in an individually-advised curriculum). Grinnell’s Biology Department has a long-standing commitment to attracting and retaining students from diverse backgrounds. Our inquiry-driven pedagogy promotes inclusivity, and we embed research and primary literature into courses from the introductory level onward, to teach students how to practice biology and explore how biology connects to other disciplines and society. We seek candidates who will teach at all levels and establish an active research program involving undergraduates. Additional information about this position, teaching and conducting research at Grinnell, our curriculum, facilities, and faculty is available at the Biology Department website . Assistant Professor (Ph.D.) preferred; Associate Professor possible. In letters of application, candidates should discuss their interest in developing as a teacher and scholar in a highly selective undergraduate liberal arts college that emphasizes close student-faculty interaction . They should also discuss their potential to contribute to a college community that maintains a diversity of people and perspectives as one of its core values. To be assured of full consideration, all application materials should be received by October 2, 2026. Candidates will need to upload the following materials: · A letter of application · Curriculum vitae · Graduate transcripts (unofficial copies are acceptable) · Statements describing teaching interests and a research program that can involve undergraduates. · Names and email contact information for three professional references Questions about this search should be directed to the search chair, Professor Vince Eckhart , at BiologySearch@grinnell.edu or 641-269-4040.",
      "department": "Grinnell College"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188740": {
      "title": "Department of Psychology (Tenure Track, Start Fall 2027)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-03 Location: Grinnell, IA The Department of Psychology invites applications for a tenure-track appointment in social psychology, broadly defined, beginning Fall 2027. Assistant Professor (Ph.D.) preferred; Instructor (ABD) or Associate Professor possible. We seek a candidate with research and teaching interests in social and/or personality psychology whose courses will engage and support our diverse undergraduate students and involve them meaningfully in research. The Department of Psychology offers one of Grinnell College’s largest majors. Our investigative curriculum equips students with strong research skills that facilitate participation in individually mentored research. Modern teaching laboratories, provide the venues for authentic investigations. Our teacher-scholar model fosters faculty student research collaborations. The teaching load is five courses [OR EQUIVALENT] over two semesters; periodically one course will be Tutorial (a topical writing/critical thinking course for first-year students, the only College-wide requirement beyond the completion of a major in an individually-advised curriculum). The successful candidates will teach some combination of introductory psychology and both intermediate and advanced courses in their area of specialization. In addition, successful candidates will mentor and engage students in authentic research projects . To support the development of a robust research program involving undergraduates, the College provides highly competitive start‑up funds, dedicated laboratory space, and a junior research leave following a successful third‑year review, in addition to regular sabbatical opportunities for tenured faculty. Additional information about our curriculum and faculty can be found at https://www.grinnell.edu/academics/majors-concentrations/psychology . Assistant Professor (Ph.D.) preferred; Instructor (ABD) or Associate Professor possible. In letters of application, candidates should discuss their interest in developing as a teacher and scholar in a highly selective undergraduate liberal arts college that emphasizes close student-faculty interaction . They should also discuss their potential to contribute to a college community that maintains a diversity of people and perspectives as one of its core values. To be assured of full consideration, all application materials should be received by October 1st. To apply, candidates will need to upload the following materials: · A letter of application · Curriculum vitae ·         A statement of teaching interests and philosophy with attention to inclusive, evidence-based pedagogy and mentoring undergraduate researchers ·         A statement on research interests that describes how undergraduates can be meaningfully involved. · Graduate transcripts (unofficial copies are acceptable) · Names and email contact information for three professional references Questions about this search should be directed to the search chair, Professor Chris Ralston , at [PSYCHOLOGYSEARCH@Grinnell.EDU] or 641-269-4040.",
      "department": "Grinnell College"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188789": {
      "title": "Department of Chemistry (Tenure Track, Start Fall 2027)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-04 Location: Grinnell, IA The Department of Chemistry invites applications for a tenure-track appointment in Organic Chemistry beginning Fall 2027. Assistant Professor (Ph.D.) or Associate Professor possible. Applicants must have a Ph.D. in Organic Chemistry, Biochemistry, Bioorganic chemistry, or a closely related field (postdoctoral experience preferred) and demonstrated commitment to excellence in teaching and research. The successful candidate will be expected to teach courses including organic chemistry, biochemistry, and electives in the candidate's area of specialization. Teaching responsibilities might also include other courses within the chemistry curriculum. In addition, the candidate is expected to develop an active research program involving undergraduate students . The teaching load is five courses over two semesters (with labs counting as a ½ course); periodically one course will be Tutorial (a topical writing/critical thinking course for first-year students, the only College-wide requirement beyond the completion of a major in an individually-advised curriculum). Additional information about our curriculum and faculty can be found at http://www.grinnell.edu/academics/areas/chemistry . Assistant Professor (Ph.D.) or Associate Professor possible. In letters of application, candidates should discuss their interest in developing as a teacher and scholar in a highly selective undergraduate liberal arts college that emphasizes close student-faculty interaction . They should also discuss their potential to contribute to a college community that maintains a diversity of people and perspectives as one of its core values. To be assured of full consideration, all application materials should be received by October 1. To apply, candidates will need to upload the following materials: · A letter of application · Curriculum vitae · Transcripts (copies are acceptable) · A statement of teaching philosophy and a description of research interests · Email addresses for three references Questions about this search should be directed to the search chair, Professor Stephen Sieck , at [ChemistrySearch@grinnell.edu] or 641-269-3172.",
      "department": "Grinnell College"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189423": {
      "title": "Department of Computer Science (Tenure Track, Start Fall 2027)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-12 Location: Grinnell, IA The Department of Computer Science invites applications for a tenure-track appointment  beginning Fall 2027. Assistant Professor (Ph.D.) preferred; Instructor (ABD) or Associate Professor possible. We are eager to consider candidates whose scholarly agenda expands the department’s existing breadth in Computer Science. Candidates with degrees in closely related fields will also be considered. The teaching load is five courses (or equivalent; some classes with a significant lab component are credited as 1.5 courses) over two semesters; periodically one course will be Tutorial (a topical writing/critical thinking course for first-year students, the only College-wide requirement beyond the completion of a major in an individually-advised curriculum). The successful candidate will be prepared and willing to teach two of the department’s three introductory courses (and eventually all three), as well as at least two of the upper-level required (non-elective) courses for the major. Additional information about our curriculum and faculty can be found at https://www.cs.grinnell.edu . Assistant Professor (Ph.D.) preferred; Instructor (ABD) or Associate Professor possible. In letters of application, candidates should discuss their interest in developing as a teacher and scholar in a highly selective undergraduate liberal arts college that emphasizes close student-faculty interaction . They should also discuss their potential to contribute to a college community that maintains a diversity of people and perspectives as one of its core values. To be assured of full consideration, all application materials should be received by October 2, 2026. To apply, candidates will need to upload the following materials: · A letter of application · Curriculum vitae · Undergraduate and graduate transcripts (unofficial copies are acceptable) · Teaching statement · Research statement · Names and email contact information for three professional references The teaching statement should describe the candidate’s pedagogical philosophy and approaches and indicate teaching interests. The research statement should discuss the candidate's scholarly work and agenda for a general computing audience. Questions about this search should be directed to the search chair, Professor Jerod Weinman , at cssearch@grinnell.edu or 641-269-3172.",
      "department": "Grinnell College"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190316": {
      "title": "Department of Mathematics (Tenure Track, Start Fall 2027)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-21 Location: Grinnell, IA The Department of Mathematics invites applications for a tenure-track appointment in mathematics beginning Fall 2027. Assistant Professor (Ph.D.) preferred; Instructor (ABD) or Associate Professor possible. Research and teaching interests are welcome from any area of mathematics, with special consideration given to candidates interested in algebra and the transition to advanced mathematical thinking. We are seeking a mathematician who is excited to work in a collaborative department committed to ensuring that mathematics is accessible to all students. Faculty members work closely together to support student learning, shape curriculum, and advance departmental initiatives. The teaching load is five courses [OR EQUIVALENT] over two semesters; periodically one course will be Tutorial (a topical writing/critical thinking course for first-year students, the only College-wide requirement beyond the completion of a major in an individually-advised curriculum). Additional information about our curriculum and faculty can be found at https://www.grinnell.edu/academics/majors-concentrations/math Assistant Professor (Ph.D.) preferred; Instructor (ABD) or Associate Professor possible. In letters of application, candidates should discuss their interest in developing as a teacher and scholar in a highly selective undergraduate liberal arts college that emphasizes close student-faculty interaction. They should also discuss their potential to contribute to a college community that maintains a diversity of people and perspectives as one of its core values. To be assured of full consideration, all application materials should be received by October 15, 2026. To apply, candidates will need to upload the following materials: · Letter of application  (should include a discussion of how the candidate will contribute to creating a sense of belonging for all students in mathematics, especially students who are underrepresented in the discipline) · Curriculum vitae · Graduate transcripts (unofficial copies are acceptable) · Teaching statement · Brief research statement (no more than 2 pages) including potential or ongoing research projects with undergraduates · Names and email contact information for three professional references Questions about this search should be directed to the search chair, Professor Jeff Blanchard, at MathSearch@grinnell.edu or 641-269-3172",
      "department": "Grinnell College"
    }
  },
